-# Agent Note: Knowledge subagent continuation
-
-Status: implemented
-
-English | [中文](2026-08-15-knowledge-continuation.zh.md)
-
-## Problem
-
-Knowledge and code searches need a user-selected scope and an upstream conversation that survives follow-up questions. Letting a parent agent call the searches directly or letting model arguments carry scope and upstream session identity would make authority and continuation depend on model-controlled data.
-
-## Decision
-
-The Chrome connector forwards the executing Harness session lineage through MCP `_meta`, not model arguments. The Knowledge Platform adapter keys its upstream `session_id` by child session, query kind, and selected scope. Scope selection remains extension-owned UI state and a changed scope naturally starts a new upstream continuation.
-
-`knowledge_subagent` is continuable and restricts its inherited tools to the two Connector searches. The parent can delegate a search but cannot call those searches directly because the Connector rejects an identity without a parent session. The Chrome MCP client also applies raw-name `toolScopes`: `code_search` and `knowledge_search` register only on spawned subagent contexts, while Office and browser tools remain global. The normal child `report` setup remains available to continuable children.
-
-## Verification
-
-Focused Connector tests prove that a continuable child identity reaches the adapter without model-controlled scope arguments. Adapter tests pin SSE completion and scope-fingerprint isolation, and the real Web Profile smoke test discovers and executes the generated Connector patch. These tests do not establish an authenticated Knowledge Platform response in a user's Chrome profile.
-
-The MCP client lifecycle test proves a parent has ordinary global tools but not the two raw searches, while a spawned subagent receives both searches. The Chrome patch test pins the two raw-name overrides.
-
-## Alternatives considered
-
-**Expose the searches to the parent agent.** Rejected because a root session has no continuable child identity, so direct calls would bypass the authority and continuity rule.
-
-**Rely only on the parent prompt and Connector rejection.** Rejected because the parent could still see and waste turns on the low-level search tools before the Connector rejects the call.
-
-**Pass scope and upstream `session_id` as model tool arguments.** Rejected because the model could select a scope or continuation identity outside the extension-owned selection.
-
-**Use a one-shot search subagent.** Rejected because every follow-up would create a new child identity and therefore a new upstream conversation.
-
-## Consequences
-
-- Follow-up searches in the same child, query kind, and selected scope reuse the upstream `session_id`.
-- Changing the selected scope starts an isolated upstream conversation.
-- The parent tool list no longer advertises low-level code or knowledge search, so it reaches them through the continuable wrapper tools.
-- The Chrome extension remains the owner of catalog retrieval, selection persistence, and authenticated service requests; ordinary Web sessions do not receive the selector.
